<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anschluss</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1938">1938</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Anschluss was the annexation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> by Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in March 1938. After Chancellor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurt-schuschnigg">Kurt Schuschnigg</a> called a referendum on independence, Hitler forced his resignation, and German troops crossed the border on 12 March to an enthusiastic reception from many Austrians. A plebiscite held under Nazi control in April recorded over 99 per cent approval. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> declared the annexation void in 1943, and Austria was restored as a separate state in 1945.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anschluss.jpg" alt="Anschluss" width="320" height="443" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Heinrich Hoffmann · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anschluss_Heldenplatz1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The idea of an Anschluss, a union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in a Greater Germany, arose after the unification of Germany in 1871 excluded Austria, and gained support after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire</a> fell in 1918. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> and the 1919 treaty of Saint Germain forbade the union. Support was strong in the 1920s, particularly on the Austrian left and centre, because many believed Austria, stripped of its imperial lands, was not viable economically. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> became German chancellor on 30 January 1933 the aim was identified with the Nazi regime. Austrian Nazis assassinated the Austrian chancellor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/engelbert-dollfuss">Engelbert Dollfuss</a> during an attempted coup in July 1934.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 12 February 1938, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berchtesgaden">Berchtesgaden</a>, under threat of invasion, Chancellor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurt-schuschnigg">Kurt Schuschnigg</a> accepted an ultimatum to appoint the Austrian Nazi Arthur Seyss-Inquart as minister of the interior. Schuschnigg announced on 9 March a referendum on independence, to be held on 13 March. On 11 March Hitler demanded its cancellation and Schuschnigg's resignation, and President Wilhelm Miklas reluctantly appointed Seyss-Inquart in his place. Seyss-Inquart invited the German army to cross the border to restore order.</p><p>The 8th Army crossed on the morning of 12 March, and the Austrian army had been ordered not to resist. Many Austrians cheered the troops, though one anti-Nazi German observer suspected the welcome was staged. Within days 70,000 people had been arrested. A referendum on 10 April officially recorded 99.7 per cent approval. Historians agree the votes were accurately counted, but the vote was neither free nor secret, and about 360,000 people, 8 per cent of the eligible voters, had lost the right to vote.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The campaign against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>'s Jews began at once. They were driven through the streets of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> and their homes and shops were plundered, and over 6,000 Jews were arrested during the Kristallnacht pogrom of 9 and 10 November. The Anschluss was among the first steps towards the Greater German Reich, followed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich-agreement">Munich Agreement</a> of September 1938 and the dismantling of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> in March 1939. The Moscow Declaration of 1943 declared the annexation null and void, and a provisional government did the same on 27 April 1945. Austria stayed under Allied control until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-state-treaty">Austrian State Treaty</a> of 1955 restored its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Anschluss” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…German-speaking lands of the fallen Habsburg monarchy and renamed by the Treaty of Saint-Germain, lasted until Engelbert Dollfuss shut down parliament in 1933; the Austrian Civil War of February 1934 outlawed the Social Democrats, and on 13 March 1938 the <b>Anschluss</b> made Austria a part of Nazi Germany.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-austria">Republic of Austria · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>It returned in 1929 with entirely new words as the anthem of Austria until the <b>Anschluss</b> of 1938.</blockquote><a href="/songs/gott-erhalte-franz-den-kaiser">Gott erhalte Franz den Kaiser</a></li><li><blockquote>The next day Austria was declared part of the German Reich, the <b>Anschluss</b> that Versailles had forbidden.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-12">12 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>German troops entered Austria on 12 March 1938, and the country was annexed to Nazi Germany in the <b>Anschluss</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/federal-state-of-austria">Federal State of Austria</a></li></ul></section>
